messages:
anna: quick heads-up — I'm hitting partial-JSON parse errors in the JS SDK when auth refresh happens mid-stream. repro: chat streaming -> token expires -> client refreshes -> server returns a cut-off chunk + 401 -> our parser throws "Unexpected end of JSON input". anyone run into this? :thinking:

anna: minimal fetch pattern i'm using:
```js
const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true) {
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, {stream: true});
  // trying to parse NDJSON/objects as they arrive
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
    // emit and clear buf...
    buf = '';
  } catch(e) {
    // keep buffering
  }
}
```

anna: sample error from client logs: `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input at JSON.parse (<anonymous>)`

mike: yep seen this pattern before. most times it's a race between the auth refresh/reconnect and the incremental parser. two suggestions: (1) don't call `JSON.parse` on partial buffers — use an incremental parser (clarinet, ndjson-stream) or a tiny stateful reassembler that looks for complete objects, (2) on 401 mid-stream treat as special signal: keep unfinished chunk in a buffer and retry the request then feed reassembled prefix to new stream. :+1:

mike: quick pseudo fix — bracket-counting reassembler (very small):
```js
function tryEmit(buffer) {
  let depth = 0;
  let start = null;
  for (let i = 0; i < buffer.length; i++) {
    if (buffer[i] === '{') { depth++; if (start === null) start = i; }
    if (buffer[i] === '}') { depth--; if (depth === 0 && start !== null) {
      const objStr = buffer.slice(start, i+1);
      yield objStr;
      // mark consumed range etc.
    }}
  }
}
```

ryan: also consider backpressure/slow-consumer side effects. if the app is busy doing token exchange on main thread the readable stream may not be drained quickly => internal chunk coalescing/truncation behaviors differ between runtimes. for Node fetch we saw similar problems when `highWaterMark` is low. try consuming via stream events and temporarily `pause()` the body while refresh happens, or increase `highWaterMark` on stream creation.

ryan: Node snippet:
```js
res.body.on('data', chunk => { buf += chunk.toString('utf8'); tryFlush(); });
res.body.pause();
// do auth refresh then resume
res.body.resume();
```

ryan: or use a Transform stream that reassembles complete events and enforces a small queue so the parser never sees half-objects. this also helps when we need to hold incomplete prefix across reconnects.

mike: another practical pattern — when you detect a 401 mid-stream, have the client emit a special `need_refresh` event to the app layer instead of trying to parse the current buffer. the app can then decide to finish/flush the buffer or keep it and attach as prefix to the next connection. server-side support for `Retry-After` or `resume-token` makes this much cleaner.

anna: thanks both. i'll try the bracket-counting reassembler + preserve prefix across reconnect. i also want to add a small backpressure test to the SDK test harness to simulate slow token exchange and ensure we don't drop/lose bytes.

